A quick stovetop marinade combining soy sauce, brown sugar, pungent garlic, grated ginger root, and toasted sesame oil. A portion is set aside before marinating to serve as a clean finishing sauce for noodles or rice.

## Ingredients

- ½ cup soy sauce (low-sodium works best)
- ¼ cup water
- ⅓ cup brown sugar (packed)
- 1 tablespoon rice vinegar or mirin
- 4 cloves fresh garlic, finely minced
- 1 tablespoon fresh ginger, grated
- 1 teaspoon toasted sesame oil

## Instructions

1. **Simmer and Dissolve:** Whisk the soy sauce, water, brown sugar, rice vinegar (or mirin), minced garlic, grated ginger, and sesame oil together in a small saucepan over medium heat. Stir continuously until the brown sugar fully dissolves and tiny bubbles begin to form around the edge of the pan. Remove from heat immediately.
2. **Set Aside Clean Portion:** Before bringing the marinade into contact with raw meat, pour ¼ cup of the warm liquid into a clean container and set aside. This clean portion will be used later as a finishing drizzle for noodles, grains, or vegetables.
3. **Cool Down:** Allow the remaining marinade in the saucepan to cool thoroughly to room temperature to prevent it from warming the raw chicken.
4. **Soak the Protein:** Place chicken thighs (or your preferred protein) into a shallow dish or zip-top bag. Pour the cooled marinade over the meat, ensuring all sides are coated. Seal or cover, and chill in the refrigerator for at least 30 minutes and up to 2 hours.

## Notes

- **Make-ahead glaze:** Marinade can be whisked together in advance and stored in an airtight jar in the refrigerator for up to 1 week.
- **Thickening into a glaze:** To reduce into a thick barbecue/stir-fry glaze, simmer the reserved portion with ½ teaspoon cornstarch dissolved in 1 teaspoon cold water until glossy and thick.
